<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ottoneum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1604">1604</a>–<a href="/years/1606">1606</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ottoneum is a building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, built from 1604 to 1606 for Landgrave Maurice of Hesse-Kassel and named after his son Otto. It was the first permanent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> building in Germany, used by the court's company of actors. It later served as an art and natural history collection and since the 19th century has housed Kassel's natural history museum.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The building was designed by the architect William Vernukken for Landgrave Moritz, whose favourite son Otto gave it its name. Its time as a cultural centre ended with the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> in 1618, and it was put to other uses. In 1696, on the orders of Charles I, Landgrave of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>, the court architect Johann Conrad Giesler converted it to hold the landgraves' art and natural history collection, with the master builder Paul du Ry carrying out the work.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In 1709 Landgrave Charles founded the Collegium Carolinum, a centre of scientific research whose scholars included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georg-forster">Georg Forster</a> and Samuel Thomas von Soemmerring, and in this period the Ottoneum also held an anatomical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and an observatory. By 1779 the collections had outgrown it and were moved to the nearby Fridericianum, where the public could see them. A century later, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, natural history objects returned to the building, and since 1928, when it passed to the city, it has been the Naturkundemuseum.</p><p>In October 1943 fire and high explosive bombs damaged the building and more than half of the collection was lost. It was rebuilt from historical plans between 1949 and 1954, and the collection has since grown through purchases and gifts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Among its holdings are the skeleton of the Goethe elephant, lent by Soemmerring to Goethe for his study of the premaxilla, whose preserved skin was lost in the wartime fire; the Ratzenberger Herbarium of 1556 to 1592, 700 pressed plants in three leather-bound volumes; and the Schildbach Wooden Library, 530 volumes made by Carl Schildbach from 1771 to 1799, each built from the parts of one kind of tree. Displays trace the region's past from the Permian to the Ice Age, with a life-size Iguanodon skeleton and a woolly mammoth.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ottoneum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>William IV was a noted astronomer, and Maurice the Learned adopted Calvinism and in 1605 built the <b>Ottoneum</b>, the first permanent theatre building in Germany.</blockquote><a href="/state/landgraviate-of-hesse-kassel">Landgraviate of Hesse-Kassel</a></li></ul></section>
